}, "tests/test_resources.py": {"bytes": 773, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "58060f00bbd9e6e647914d2a16646739dedc44ed2ad3b93299c0619bc1765f9e"}, "tests/test_retry.py": {"bytes": 833, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "7388276b35bdd43be48f2c535684c90d887800a4ccb2c019d75c01957062b731"}, "tests/test_statemachine.py": {"bytes": 567, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "bf425d22872d903161195e51bbaca57dea3e5e8e4b23cbd77ee26ddc578e74ef"}}, "child_edit_calls": 0, "child_plan_reminder": false, "cmd": ["/workspace/openbench-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/2946a6d9a78466c19637cb75473769b1deb6f03e/opencode", "run", "--print-logs", "-m", "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5", "--format", "json", "--title", "openbench", "Start by using the grep tool to search for `self` across the project.\n\n# Fix the failing tests in `taskflow`\n\n`taskflow/` is an in-memory job-orchestration engine: it runs a pipeline of\ninterdependent tasks to completion on a virtual clock, with priority\nscheduling, retries with backoff, resource-limited concurrency, a per-job state\nmachine, an event bus, and an event-sourced run history. The package has a\n`tests/` suite that describes how all of this is supposed to behave.\n\nRight now the suite is failing in several places. Run it from this directory:\n\n```\npython3 -m unittest\n```\n\nWork through the failures and fix the code under `taskflow/` until the whole\nsuite passes. Treat the tests as the specification \u2014 they encode the intended\nbehavior; do not modify them.\n\nA few things worth knowing before you dive in:\n\n- **The failures can be misleading.** A test named for one module may be\n  failing because of a defect in a *different* module. Read each failure for\n  what it actually asserts, then trace the behavior back to its root cause\n  rather than patching the nearest line.\n- **Fixing one bug can uncover another.** Some defects are masked by others:\n  correcting the first makes a second one visible (a test that was failing for\n  reason A starts failing for reason B). Expect to fix, re-run, and re-diagnose\n  more than once.\n- **The defects are genuine logic errors**, not typos that raise on import. The\n  code reads as intended-but-wrong: comments and docstrings describe the\n  correct behavior, so compare what each function *says* it does against what it\n  *actually* does.\n\nDone when `python3 -m unittest` reports every test passing.\n"], "completed": true, "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 7, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "stdout", "first_after_build": true, "first_side": "without", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "opencode", "harness_version": "0.0.0--202610200433 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/2946a6d9a78466c19637cb75473769b1deb6f03e/opencode)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": true, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": null, "model_max_tokens": null, "output_tail": "d not very useful. I'll run the tests now.\n[tool: bash]\nStart with the small modules.\n[tool: read]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: bash]\nTwo clear bugs: queue sort key and should_retry.\n[tool: read]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: read]\n[tool: edit]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: edit]\nTerminal events aren't reaching history. Look at events and history.\n[tool: read]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: edit]\nAll 25 tests now pass with `python3 -m unittest`. I didn't touch the tests.\n\nI fixed seven bugs, and none of them were in the module the failing test was named for:\n\n- **`queue.py`**: `_Entry.sort_key` returned `(seq, -priority)`. It now returns `(-priority, seq)`, so higher priority goes first and ties break by insertion order.\n- **`retry.py`**: `should_retry` used `attempts_made < max_attempts - 1`, which cut retries short by one. It is now `attempts_made < max_attempts`.\n- **`config.py`**: `deep_merge` recursed with its arguments swapped, so the defaults overrode a task's own nested `retry` and `resources` settings. It now merges `result[key]` with `value`.\n- **`resources.py`**: `ResourcePool.release` subtracted 1 instead of `cost`. Any task with a cost above 1 leaked capacity, which is why the resource tests never finished.\n- **`statemachine.py`**: `batch_order_ok` had its comparison backwards (`>=`). Within a batch, priorities must be non-increasing, so it is now `current_priority <= prev_priority`.\n- **`scheduler.py`**: `_cascade_skip` only skipped direct dependents. It now uses `transitive_dependents`, so a permanent failure skips everything downstream.\n- **`events.py`**: `_prefix_matches` tested `prefix.startswith(topic + \".\")`, which is reversed. It is now `topic.startswith(prefix + \".\")`. Without this, the history never received terminal events.\n\nThe `grep` for `self` you asked for first returned only a truncated sample of matches, so I ran the tests directly and worked from the failures.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 8, "replies_ok": null, "replies_throttled": null, "requests_cache_read": 373296, "requests_cache_write": 89324, "requests_cost_usd": 0.2975836, "requests_count": 14, "requests_input_uncached": 32, "requests_output": 3688, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "opencode:trig-grep-trunc:claude-opus-5-5:trial1", "run_seed": 1016, "sampling_observed": null, "schedule_index": 4, "score": 1.0, "started_at": "2026-10-20T04:33:27.107566+00:00", "subagent_resumed": false, "subagent_write_calls": 0, "success": true, "t_agent_s": 43.012, "t_checker_s": 0.244, "t_env_setup_s": 0.